<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    A parameter the calling convention passes in a floating-point register is
    a float of that register's width (kuna_floatreg.rs, float_input_vote).
    A strict correction: there is no option, and the one pass is the fix.

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. The script loads
    three in-tree fixtures built from floatparam.c (source beside them): gcc
    -O0 and clang -O2 for x86-64, and clang -O2 for AArch64.

    fs and ff store a double and a float through a pointer, fr into a field,
    and sink two floats into an array. Nothing computes with the value, so the
    fold typed it raw bytes and the prototype printed an integer, which the
    convention passes in a general register: fs(unsigned long a0, ..) moved
    the double from xmm0 (d0) to rdi (x0). The register makes it a float.

    The refusals: bits hands the double back in rax (x0) and keeps its integer
    type. fy copies its double into a global: with floatglobals off every
    global refuses, and fy keeps its integer type; on, the whole-object scan
    finds nothing else touching the global and fy takes the double.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
